## Chapter 18: Testing & Fuzzing

Testing in Zig is not an afterthought bolted on by a third-party framework—it is a first‑class language feature. The compiler itself understands test blocks, and the standard library provides all the machinery needed for assertions, leak‑checking allocators, and even simple fuzz testing. This chapter shows how Zig’s approach to testing eliminates hidden behaviour, keeps test code close to the production logic, and gives you the same fine‑grained control you expect from the rest of the language.

---

### 1. Basic Usage

A test is a named block introduced with the `test` keyword. You can place these blocks anywhere a normal declaration is allowed—at file scope, inside a struct, or even inside a function (though that is rare). To execute them, you run `zig test <file.zig>`.

```zig
const std = @import("std");

test "basic arithmetic" {
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(i32, 7), 3 + 4);
}
```

If the block performs operations that can fail, declare it as returning an error union:

```zig
test "parse integer" !void {
    const x = try std.fmt.parseInt(u16, "42", 10);
    try std.testing.expectEqual(@as(u16, 42), x);
}
```

`zig test` compiles all the test blocks in the given root file (and any modules it imports) into a single test runner binary, then executes it. Each test block becomes a separate function inside that runner. A test passes if it returns normally; it fails if it returns an error or triggers a panic.

#### The Testing Allocator

`std.testing.allocator` is a global `GeneralPurposeAllocator` configured to detect double‑frees and memory leaks. Use it in every test that performs allocation so you never accidentally ship a leak.

```zig
test "concatenate strings" !void {
    const ally = std.testing.allocator;
    const result = try std.mem.concat(ally, u8, &.{ "Hello, ", "world!" });
    defer ally.free(result);
    try std.testing.expectEqualStrings("Hello, world!", result);
}
```

The test runner will report any memory that was allocated but not freed. This makes `std.testing.allocator` your first line of defence against the class of bugs that C’s `valgrind` and Rust’s `#[global_allocator]` tricks catch.

#### Assertion Helpers

The `std.testing` module provides a family of expectation functions, all returning `!void` so they compose cleanly with `try`:

| Function | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `expect(ok)` | Fail if `ok` is `false` |
| `expectEqual(expected, actual)` | Deep equality via `std.meta.eql` |
| `expectEqualDeep(expected, actual)` | Recursive structural equality |
| `expectEqualSlices(T, a, b)` | Compare slices element‑by‑element |
| `expectError(expected_err, actual_err_union)` | Check a specific error |
| `expectFmt(expected_str, comptime fmt, args...)` | Match formatted output |
| `expectApproxEqAbs(expected, actual, tolerance)` | Absolute float tolerance |
| `expectApproxEqRel(expected, actual, tolerance)` | Relative float tolerance |

#### Table‑Driven Tests

Zig does not provide a parameterized test decorator; instead, you write a simple loop over a slice of test cases. This is explicit, zero‑magic, and trivially composable with comptime.

```zig
test "URL parsing" !void {
    const cases = [_]struct {
        input: []const u8,
        scheme: []const u8,
        host: []const u8,
    }{
        .{ .input = "https://ziglang.org", .scheme = "https", .host = "ziglang.org" },
        .{ .input = "ftp://example.com/",  .scheme = "ftp",   .host = "example.com"  },
    };
    for (cases) |tc| {
        const parsed = try parseUrl(tc.input);
        defer parsed.deinit();
        try std.testing.expectEqualStrings(tc.scheme, parsed.scheme);
        try std.testing.expectEqualStrings(tc.host, parsed.host);
    }
}
```

#### Fuzz Testing

`std.testing.fuzz` takes a user‑provided function that accepts a `[]const u8` (random bytes) and runs it many times with different inputs. If the function returns an error or panics, the fuzz run fails. This is a simple *dumb* fuzzer—no coverage guidance—but it catches the most egregious crashes with minimal ceremony.

```zig
test "fuzz integer parser" !void {
    try std.testing.fuzz(struct {
        fn testOne(input: []const u8) anyerror!void {
            _ = std.fmt.parseInt(i64, std.mem.trim(u8, input, &std.ascii.whitespace), 10) catch return;
        }
    }.testOne, .{});
}
```

